{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d39f9239-c8c2-459e-b15a-f9d245f2701e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b643a2193c80038b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-98wjqx0d/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E008.md","content":"# maas-api observability instrumentation and Perses dashboards\n\n## Description\n\nInstrument maas-api with the Prometheus metrics and structured logging required for the MaaS xKS observability value proposition, and author the Perses dashboards that render those metrics. Metrics are P0 (token usage, rate limiting enforcement, API key lifecycle, inference latency); structured JSON logging in maas-api is P1 (authentication decisions, API key validation). maas-api already exposes a Prometheus metrics listener and exports traces via OpenTelemetry (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md), so this extends existing foundations.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add/extend Prometheus metrics in maas-api for:\n  - Token usage per user/subscription.\n  - Rate limiting enforcement events (429 responses with exceeded quota).\n  - API key lifecycle events (creation, validation, revocation).\n  - Inference request latency.\n- Emit structured JSON logs from maas-api for authentication decisions (success/failure, method, reason) and API key validation events, formatted for aggregation in CKS Grafana Loki.\n- Author Perses dashboards for MaaS: token usage per subscription, rate limiting enforcement events, API key lifecycle metrics, and inference latency.\n- Keep metric emission asynchronous to avoid observability latency overhead (strategy risk mitigation: if p99 overhead exceeds 5ms, move emission to post-response goroutines).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with Prometheus integration, when a user creates an API key, makes inference requests, and exceeds a rate limit, then Prometheus metrics include `maas_api_key_created_total`, `maas_inference_requests_total{user, model, subscription}`, and `maas_rate_limit_enforced_total{user, model, exceeded_quota}`, measured by querying the CKS Prometheus instance.\n- Given structured logging enabled, when an authentication event occurs (success or failure), then the log entry includes timestamp, user identity, authentication method (OIDC/API key), decision (allow/deny), and reason (if denied), measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki with label filters.\n- Given Perses dashboards installed, when an admin navigates to the MaaS observability dashboard, then the dashboard displays token usage per subscription, rate limiting enforcement events, and API key lifecycle metrics over the last 24 hours, measured by dashboard rendering with live data.\n- Observability overhead does not degrade request latency beyond the strategy's threshold (async emission; p99 overhead ≤ 5ms or moved to post-response goroutines).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] Observability metrics functional: token usage, rate limiting enforcement, API key lifecycle events (per Staff Engineer Input guidance embedded in the strategy) — instrumentation portion.\n- [P1] Observability logging functional: authentication events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection — maas-api portion.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Observability instrumentation in maas-api does not require MaaS 3.4 GA code changes — \"Needs validation\" per the strategy: if maas-api is frozen, the instrumentation lands as a post-3.4 patch, or logging is deferred to P1. The epic exists either way; only landing timing changes.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":69689,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87fd07bf831150f8","agent_id":"a20fa1aa5b3a9ab51","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"e960db67-4bc4-41b9-9ced-3353141515ff","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:33:20.679Z","s