{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8332f15412a6e50d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 15\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8003 Decomposition — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: validate MaaS deployment on xKS (CKS priority, then AKS) as a Dev Preview, with external OIDC authentication replacing OpenShift identity infrastructure, Helm-based installation replacing OLM, and observability (metrics, logging, tracing) as a core value proposition.\n13\t\n14\t**Staff Engineer Input note:** The Staff Engineer Input section of the strategy contains only template placeholder text (no authored content), so no separate overrides were parsed. However, the strategy body explicitly embeds prior staff engineer guidance — CKS as priority platform, observability as core value proposition, and RHCL xKS support as a strategic dependency requiring Sprint 1 validation — and that guidance was treated as primary direction throughout.\n15\t\n16\t**Step 0 triage:** Not below-threshold (strategy is M-sized, spans multiple components and three teams, with multiple architectural sub-systems: controller code, API code, Helm packaging, observability, cross-team validation). Not docs-only (substantive code and configuration changes required). Full decomposition applies.\n17\t\n18\t## Component Mappings\n19\t\n20\t| Strategy component | Canonical component (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) | Notes |\n21\t|---|---|---|\n22\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing) | Model as a Service | Direct match; component exists in architecture context (`models-as-a-service.md`) |\n23\t| Kuadrant (Authorino, Limitador) | Inference Gateway | No canonical \"Kuadrant\" component; Kuadrant provides gateway-level authn/authz and rate limiting for the MaaS/AI gateway — closest parent-level component. Logged as non-obvious mapping |\n24\t| Observability (Prometheus, Perses, Loki) | Model and Agent Observability | MaaS-specific observability work; closest canonical match |\n25\t| RHAII installation tooling (xKS Platform) | AI Core Platform | RHAII xKS deployment tooling is not in the component list; closest parent-level platform component. Logged as non-obvious mapping |\n26\t\n27\t**Component existence (architecture context):** `models-as-a-service.md` exists (in platform). Kuadrant/RHCL has no architecture-context file of its own (referenced within `models-as-a-service.md`, `PLATFORM.md`, `ai-gateway-operator.md`) — it is an external RHCL/OpenShift-owned dependency, which is why the RHCL xKS support question is modeled as a blocking Investigation rather than an onboarding chain. Gateway API on CKS is confirmed available (Istio on CoreWeave). CKS Prometheus/Loki is assumed but unvalidated — modeled as a blocking Investigation.\n28\t\n29\t## Epic List\n30\t\n31\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n32\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 | RHCL official xKS support status and deployment mechanism validation | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 | External OIDC integration feasibility on the MaaS 3.4 codebase | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 | CKS observability infrastructure accessibility validation | Investigation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 | MaaS Helm chart packaging for xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 | maas-api external OIDC integration and OpenShift-assumption removal | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | maas-controller configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) deployment on CKS | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | maas-api observability instrumentation and Perses dashboards | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | CKS observability integration (Prometheus, Loki, Perses) | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | Structured logging for Authorino and Limitador | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P1 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | MaaS on CKS full-stack deployment validation | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E012 | MaaS on AKS deployment validation | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E013 | Distributed tracing through the MaaS request path | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E014 | MaaS Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS installation tooling | Implementation | xKS Platform | P1 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E015 | MaaS xKS documentation: known limitations and user identity flow | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n48\t\n49\tE015 priority note: `docs-authoring` priority exception applied — derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Blocker` (Blocker ≥ Critical → P0), not from its underlying P1/P2 HLRs, and it does not propagate upstream.\n50\t\n51\t## Dependency DAG\n52\t\n53\t```mermaid\n54\tgraph TD\n55\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 RHCL xKS support Investigation]\n56\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 OIDC feasibility Investigation]\n57\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 CKS observability Investigation]\n58\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 Helm chart packaging]\n59\t    E008[RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 maas-api observability]\n60\t    E001 --> E007\n61\t    E001 --> E010\n62\t    E002 --> E005\n63\t    E002 --> E006\n64\t    E003 --> E009\n65\t    E004 --> E011\n66\t    E004 --> E014\n67\t    E005 --> E011\n68\t    E006 --> E011\n69\t    E007 --> E011\n70\t    E008 --> E009\n71\t    E009 --> E015\n72\t    E010 --> E015\n73\t    E011 --> E009\n74\t    E011 --> E012\n75\t    E011 --> E013\n76\t    E012 --> E015\n77\t    E013 --> E015\n78\t    E014 --> E015\n79\t    E004 --> E015\n80\t    E005 --> E015\n81\t    E006 --> E015\n82\t    E007 --> E015\n83\t    E008 --> E015\n84\t    E011 --> E015\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tRoots: E001, E002, E003, E004, E008 (all start immediately). Critical path: E001 → E007 → E011 → E012 → E015 (length 5; the E002 → E005/E006 → E011 → E012 → E015 chains are also length 5).\n88\t\n89\t## DAG Justification\n90\t\n91\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n92\t|---|---|---|\n93\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | RHCL support commitment determines the Kuadrant deployment mechanism (RHCL Helm charts vs standalone Authorino+Limitador vs direct Envoy ext_authz). True gate, action=rewrite. |\n94\t| E001 → E010 | Rule 3 | The selected deployment mechanism determines where structured logging for auth/rate-limit enforcement is configured. True gate, action=rewrite. |\n95\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | Whether the existing MaaS AuthPolicy can reuse external OIDC as-is or needs changes determines the maas-api change scope. True gate, action=rewrite. |\n96\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 | If the Tenant CRD is frozen in MaaS 3.4 GA, the groupClaimPath code change is dropped in favor of documenting the required claim format. True gate, action=remove. |\n97\t| E003 → E009 | Rule 3 / Rule 22 | If CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, the integration epic grows provisioning scope. True gate, action=add_remediation. |\n98\t| E004 → E011 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumer) | CKS validation deploys the Helm chart produced by the packaging epic — the chart is a build-against artifact. |\n99\t| E004 → E014 | Rule 12/13 | RHAII xKS installation tooling consumes the Helm chart artifact. |\n100\t| E005 → E011 | Rule 12 | CKS validation validates the OIDC-integrated maas-api build. |\n101\t| E006 → E011 | Rule 12 | CKS validation validates group-claim-driven tier mapping. |\n102\t| E007 → E011 | Rule 16 (external dependency gates Tier 2) | Full-stack validation requires working auth/rate-limiting enforcement on CKS. |\n103\t| E008 → E009 | Rule 12 | CKS integration scrapes the metrics that instrumentation em