o structured JSON logs from maas-api, Authorino, and Limitador are queryable in CKS Grafana Loki with the required label scheme.\n45\t- Deploy the MaaS Perses dashboards (authored by the MaaS observability instrumentation work) against the CKS Prometheus instance.\n46\t- If the CKS infrastructure validation found Prometheus/Loki not accessible for MaaS, provision a dedicated Prometheus and Loki stack for the MaaS namespace instead (gate remediation scope).\n47\t- Validate the full observability data path against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- MaaS metrics endpoints are scraped by the CKS Prometheus instance (or the provisioned dedicated stack), and the MaaS metric series are queryable.\n52\t- Structured logs from maas-api, Authorino, and Limitador are queryable in CKS Grafana Loki with label filters (timestamp, user identity, authentication method, decision, reason).\n53\t- The MaaS Perses dashboard renders token usage per subscription, rate limiting enforcement events, and API key lifecycle metrics with live data over the last 24 hours.\n54\t- Any provisioning delta (dedicated Prometheus/Loki) is complete and documented if the shared CKS infrastructure was not accessible.\n55\t- License scan of any provisioned observability images passes or exceptions are documented (strategy prerequisite: License Validation pending human review).\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability\n58\t\n59\t- [P0] Observability metrics functional: token usage, rate limiting enforcement, API key lifecycle events (per Staff Engineer Input guidance embedded in the strategy) — CKS integration portion.\n60\t- [P1] Observability logging functional: authentication events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection — CKS Loki aggregation portion.\n61\t\n62\t## Dependencies and Gating\n63\t\n64\t- Gated by the CKS observability infrastructure validation: if CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, this epic's scope grows to provisioning a dedicated 