{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a243d9c17808fdf2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing edge E010 -> E009: E009 (CKS observability integration, P0)\n10\t    has ACs requiring Authorino/Limitador structured logs queryable in CKS Grafana\n11\t    Loki, but only E010 (P1) produces those logs and E010 is on no path into E009\n12\t    (deps: E003, E008, E011), so E009 can complete before E010 starts. Add E010 to\n13\t    E009 dependencies and diagram; also resolve the resulting P1-blocks-P0-AC tension\n14\t    (move the P1 logging AC out of E009 or re-prioritize E010).'\n15\t- severity: major\n16\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n17\t  description: 'Missing edge E003 -> E013: E013 configures trace export to an OpenTelemetry\n18\t    collector endpoint whose availability is confirmed by the E003 investigation (cited\n19\t    in E003 scope and assumptions), but E013 depends only on E011 and E003 is not\n20\t    upstream of it. Add E003 to E013 dependencies with gated_by/gate_failure_impact,\n21\t    or re-scope E013 to provision its own collector.'\n22\t- severity: minor\n23\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n24\t  description: 'E014 wires Kuadrant prerequisite ordering into the RHAII installation\n25\t    tooling, but the Kuadrant deployment mechanism is selected by E001/E007 and E014\n26\t    (deps: E004 only) has no path from either. State explicitly that the tooling encodes\n27\t    the fallback ladder as configurable prerequisites, or add the edge.'\n28\t- severity: minor\n29\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n30\t  description: 'Boundary edge case: the Authorino/Limitador -> CKS Loki log-shipping\n31\t    configuration is scoped by both E009 (xKS Platform) and E010 (RHCL/OpenShift)\n32\t    with the label scheme only informally agreed; ownership of the shipping config\n33\t    is ambiguous and unordered. Clarify which epic owns shipping for RHCL components\n34\t    logs.'\n35\t- severity: minor\n36\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n37\t  description: 'E013 open_questions=0 and external_dependency=0 are arguable: its\n38\t    own rationale says OTel collector availability is resolved by the E003 gating\n39\t    investigation (which has not run and has no DAG edge to E013), while every other\n40\t    investigation-gated epic scores open_questions=-1 and E009 scores external_dependency=-1\n41\t    for the same CKS infrastructure class.'\n42\t- severity: minor\n43\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n44\t  description: E008 and E009 bundle the P1 logging HLR portion with P0 metrics work\n45\t    in the same epic. Acceptable since logging rides the same instrumentation/aggregation\n46\t    surface, but it places P1-scope ACs inside P0 epics, which is what makes the missing\n47\t    E010->E009 edge priority-relevant.\n48\t- severity: minor\n49\t  criterion: Completeness\n50\t  description: The strategy PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation (in-memory LRU cache,\n51\t    TTL 5min, for validated API key hashes; the failure-mode AC references a cache\n52\t    miss scenario) is owned by no epic - E011 mentions it only as if implemented.\n53\t    Assign it to an epic (likely E005 or E011) or explicitly defer it.\n54\terror: null\n55\t---\n56\t## Review Summary\n57\t\n58\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n59\tRecommendation: revise\n60\t\n61\tThe decomposition is structurally sound in most dimensions — HLR coverage is complete, epic types and gating metadata are correct, ACs are testable, and the diagram/frontmatter DAG is internally consistent (25 edges, no cycles, no invalid references). It fails on DAG coherence: epic content reveals data dependencies that the DAG does not capture. The most serious is E009 (CKS observability integration, P0), whose acceptance criteria require Authorino/Limitador structured logs to be queryable in CKS Grafana Loki — logs that only E010 (P1, structured logging for Authorino/Limitador) produces — yet E010 is not upstream of E009 in the DAG (verified: no path E010 → E009). E009 could be scheduled and completed before E010 starts, making its own ACs unmeetable.\n62\t\n63\t## Criterion Details\n64\t\n65\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n66\tAll 13 HLRs (8 P0, 3 P1, 2 P2) map to at least one epic in the traceability matrix; spot-checking each epic's HLR Traceability section confirms the matrix is accurate, including split coverage (e.g., the P0 observability-metrics HLR split across E003/E008/E009). Priority inheritance is correct everywhere except where the DAG defect masks it (see Criterion 2): E011 (P0) is blocked only by P0 epics; E009's P0 portion does not require E010's P1 work. E015's P0 priority is correctly derived from the strategy's `priority: Blocker` via the docs-authoring exception, and flagged as a health warning — not a violation. Minor note: E008 and E009 each bundle the P1 logging HLR portion with P0 metrics work; since the logging rides the same instrumentation/aggregation surface in the same component and team, this is not priority collapse with deferrable features, but the P1 logging ACs sitting inside P0 epics is what makes the Criterion 2 edge defect priority-relevant.\n67\t\n68\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n69\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: all 25 edges match exactly in both directions, arrows run dependency → dependent, no cycles, no invalid epic references, roots (E001, E002, E003, E004, E008) match empty dependency lists, and critical path (E001 → E007 → E011 → E012 → E015, 5 nodes) is within the M-size heuristic. No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied; E004's image references are correctly handled as AC gates, not DAG edges. Conditional branches were reasonably rejected per the fallback-ladder rationale.\n70\t\n71\tHowever, content-discoverable dependencies are missing (reachability verified programmatically):\n72\t\n73\t- **Critical — missing edge E010 → E009.** E009's scope is \"Configure log shipping/aggregation so structured JSON logs from maas-api, Authorino, and Limitador are queryable in CKS Grafana Loki\", and its AC requires exactly that. E010 is the only epic that produces structured Authorino/Limitador logs. E009's dependencies are E003, E008, E011 — E010 is on no path into E009, so E009 can complete before E010 starts and its Loki AC for Authorino/Limitador logs cannot be met. The interlock runs both ways: E010's ACs are \"measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki with label filters\", which requires E009's aggregation. Fix: add E010 to E009's `dependencies` (with the diagram edge), and consider whether E009's P1 logging AC should move or E010 should be re-prioritized so a P1 epic does not block a P0 epic's acceptance criteria.\n74\t- **Major — missing edge E003 → E013.** E013's scope configures trace export to \"an OpenTelemetry collector endpoint available on CKS (availability confirmed by the CKS observability infrastructure validation)\" — it consumes E003's validation record, and its Assumptions section says collector availability is \"flagged in the CKS observability infrastructure validation\". Yet E013 depends only on E011, and E003 is on no path into E013. Either add E003 to E013's dependencies (with appropriate `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`, since the outcome changes E013's scope) or re-scope E013 to provision its own collector.\n75\t- **Minor — E014 has no path from E001/E007.** E014 wires \"prerequisite ordering (Kuadrant, Gateway API implementation, PostgreSQL, cert-manager)\" into the RHAII installation tooling, but the Kuadrant deployment mechanism is selected by E001 (validated by E007), and E014 depends only on E004. Defensible if the tooling encodes the bounded fallback ladder as configurable prerequisites — state that explicitly, or add the edge.\n76\t\n77\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n78\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated (MaaS: E002/E004/E005/E006/E008/E011/E012/E013/E015; RHCL/OpenShift: E001/E007/E010; xKS Platform: E003/E009/E014), matching the strategy's impacted-teams table, and non-obvious component mappings (Kuadrant → Inference Gateway, observability → Model and Agent Observability, tooling → AI Core Platform) are canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt` and explicitly logged. E011/E012 both validate core functionality but on different platforms — that is the strategy's explicit \"AKS AND CKS\" requirement, not duplication. No epic is clearly oversized; E004 and E011 are chunky but single-deliverable. One boundary edge case: the Authorino/Limitador → CKS Loki log pipeline is scoped by both E009 (\"Configure log shipping/aggregation so … Authorino, and Limitador … are queryable in CKS Grafana Loki\", xKS Platform) and E010 (\"Configure log output so entries are shipped to CKS Grafana Loki\", RHCL/OpenShift), with the label scheme only informally \"agreed with\" the other epic. Emission vs aggregation is a legitimate split, but ownership of the shipping configuration itself is ambiguous between two teams with no DAG edge ordering them (the Criterion 2 defect). Clarify which epic owns the shipping config for RHCL components' logs.\n79\t\n80\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n81\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 gates E007 (rewrite) and E010 (rewrite); E002 gates E005 (rewrite) and E006 (remove); E003 gates E009 (add_remediation). Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E005←E002, E006←E002, E007←E001, E009←E003, E010←E001), every gate has both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated with substantive content, and no epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts; E015 is correctly `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. The E013/E003 gap is scored under Criterion 2 (the dependency itself is missing, not just its gating metadata).\n82\t\n83\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n84\tSignal fields are type-correct everywhere (Investigations E001-E003 carry `investigation_signals`; Implementations E004-E015 carry `ai_signals`; no crossover), all 15 ai-signals files exist, and rationales are substantive and generally match frontmatter values — e.g., E001's `source_accessibility: 0` / `human_judgment_required: -2` correctly captures that the RHCL commitment is a pending human decision, and E007's low scores match its genuinely greenfield, externally-gated scope. Two arguable values, both in E013: (a) `open_questions: 0` with the rationale \"OTel collector availability is confirmed by the CKS observability infrastructure validation; no approach-changing unknown\" — but E003 is an open investigation that exists precisely because availability is unconfirmed, and every other investigation-gated epic (E005, E006, E008, E009, E010, E011) scores `open_questions: -1` for exactly this situation; (b) `external_dependency: 0` while depending on CKS-hosted collector infrastructure, where E009 scores `-1` for the same class of dependency. Also note the rationale asserts a gate (\"resolved by the gating investigation\") that does not exist in the DAG — consistent with the Criterion 2 missing edge.\n85\t\n86\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n87\tEvery epic has testable ACs, largely lifted faithfully from the strategy's Given/When/Then criteria with \"measured by\" clauses. Rule-mandated ACs are present: backwards-compatibility/config-driven ACs on the replacement epics (E004 \"No changes to RHOAI MaaS GA installation paths\", E005 \"No behavioral change to RHOAI MaaS GA\", E006 default claim path preserves existing behavior), technical-review AC on docs-authoring E015, and build-pipeline-green plus license-scan ACs on E004/E007/E009. All four strategy failure-mode criteria are covered (OIDC outage: E005/E011/E012; Limitador termination: E007; TLS rotation: E004/E011/E012; PostgreSQL unavailability: E011/E012). No epic body references siblings by draft ID — cross-references use descriptive names throughout.\n88\t\n89\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n90\tFull scope walk shows no primary gaps: deployment validation (CKS/AKS), OIDC, API keys, group claim path, rate limiting, RHCL status, metrics/logging/tracing, tier management, known-limitations and identity-flow docs, Helm build pipeline, license validation, and RHAII tooling integration all map to epics; strategy risks and assumptions are carried into epic Risks/Assumptions sections (3.4 build timing in E011, freeze risk in E008, RHCL escalation in E001/E007), and the AKS observability exclusion is explicitly justified against the strategy's CKS-named ACs rather than silently dropped. Two minor gaps: (a) the strategy's PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation — \"Implement in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5min) for validated API key hashes\" — is owned by no epic; E011 mentions it only as \"if implemented\", and the strategy's failure-mode AC references a \"cache miss scenario\" that presumes the cache exists; (b) the strategy open question \"OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages?\" is only implicitly answered by E005's OIDC-outage failure-mode AC. Neither breaks core scope.\n91\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dc1998c3ea04559","agent_id":"ad6f88bf8523a0ee8","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"ecfb2019-ca97-465c-820d-b99f7ff918e1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:47:20.433Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a243d9c17808fdf2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
