r those metric names. |\n103\t| E011 → E009 | Rule 12 | Observability integration ACs are measured against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS (\"Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with Prometheus integration...\"). |\n104\t| E011 → E012 | Strategy sequencing | Strategy: validate on CKS first (priority platform), then AKS. AKS validation reuses the validated chart, OIDC configuration, and Kuadrant mechanism. |\n105\t| E011 → E013 | Rule 12 | Tracing requires the full request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) running on CKS. |\n106\t| E004…E014 → E015 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built and validated. Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n107\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ E005/E006 | Rules 14/15, 13 | Helm packaging and OIDC code changes are parallel work streams; image references are configuration references filled at deploy time (AC gates, not DAG edges). |\n108\t| (no edge) E008 ∥ E004/E005/E006 | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas (observability instrumentation vs packaging vs auth path) — coordination risk only, not dependency. |\n109\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002/E003/E004/E008 | Rule 17 / Tier 1 | Tier 1 independent work proceeds while the RHCL dependency is resolved. |\n110\t\n111\t**Conditional branches considered and rejected:** The RHCL deployment mechanism has three bounded outcomes (RHCL Helm charts / standalone Authorino+Limitador / direct Envoy ext_authz), but the strategy frames them as a priority-ordered fallback ladder whose deliverable — working Authorino + Limitador auth and rate limiting on CKS — is identical in every outcome; only the mechanism differs. Per Rule 21's fallback principle, the fallbacks are recorded as acceptance criteria and `gate_failure_impact` on E007 rather than as separate branch epics. This also avoids dead-locking the main-plan CKS validation epic (which needs the deployment under every outcome) against non-main-plan branch nodes.\n112\t\n113\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n114\t\n115\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n116\t|---|---|---|\n117\t| MaaS successfully deployed on CKS (priority platform) | P0 | E004, E011 (E014 supports) |\n118\t| MaaS successfully deployed on AKS (\"AKS AND CKS\") | P0 | E012 |\n119\t| Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | E007, E011 |\n120\t| External OIDC authentication validated for user identity | P0 | E002, E005, E011 |\n121\t| API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E005, E011 |\n122\t| Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E002, E006 |\n123\t| RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001, E007 |\n124\t| Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E003, E008, E009 |\n125\t| Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E006, E011 |\n126\t| Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E008 (maas-api), E010 (Authorino/Limitador), E009 (Loki aggregation) |\n127\t| Known limitations documented | P1 | E015 |\n128\t| User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E015 |\n129\t| Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E013 |\n130\t\n131\tAll 13 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n132\t\n133\t## Tiered Delivery\n134\t\n135\tThe RHCL official xKS support dependency has uncertain timing (external team commitment, Sprint 1 validation). Partial value is deliverable without it, so the strategy splits into:\n136\t\n137\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts immediately):** E002, E003, E004, E005, E006, E008, E014 — Helm packaging, OIDC integration code, observability instrumentation, and the investigations themselves. These deliver risk-reduction and the xKS-ready MaaS artifacts regardless of the RHCL outcome.\n138\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E007, E010, E011, E012, E013, E015 — Kuadrant deployment on CKS, structured logging for RHCL components, full-stack CKS/AKS validation, tracing, and final documentation. Gated (directly or transitively) on E001.\n139\t\n140\tThe MaaS 3.4 GA stable-build timing (internal dependency, \"critical blocker\" for Summit) does not split tiers: no work can be validated without a build, and the strategy's mitigations (RC build by April 27, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are timing tactics, not structural alternatives. Recorded as a health warning and an acceptance-criteria gate on the validation epics.\n141\t\n142\t## Health Warnings\n143\t\n144\t1. **Docs epic priority inversion (deliberate):** E015 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Blocker` → P0) even though its underlying HLRs are P1 (known limitations) and P2 (identity flow). Flagged for human review of whether a P0 docs epic is intended for a Dev Preview.\n145\t2. **MaaS 3.4 GA build availability:** code freeze passed late March 2026; stable-build delivery timing is unknown and is a Summit-timeline risk. Not structurally modelable — recorded as an AC gate (use RC build; RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) on E011/E012.\n146\t3. **Pending process gates:** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding (Helm chart build pipeline), License Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy prerequisites. Helm chart build pipeline onboarding is an AC on E004; license verification of pulled images (Authorino, Limitador, Perses) is an AC on E007/E009; the rest are program-level gates outside Dev Preview scope per the strategy.\n147\t4. **Observability instrumentation freeze risk:** if maas-api is frozen at 3.4 GA, metrics instrumentation may need a post-3.4 patch or deferral of logging to P1 (strategy assumption \"Needs validation\"). Recorded as an assumption/AC on E008 rather than an Investigation — the epic exists either way; only landing timing changes.\n148\t5. **AKS GatewayClass choice** (`envoy-gateway` vs `azure-application-gateway`) is an implementation detail deferred to E012 — not a genuine unknown (either works; the strategy lists both as validated options).\n149\t6. **Shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL** for MaaS on xKS is a configuration decision (xKS Platform owns); captured as a configurable Helm value and AC on E004, not an Investigation.\n150\t\n151\t## Investigation Gate Summary\n152\t\n153\t| Investigation | Gated epics | Gate action | Fallback approach |\n154\t|---|---|---|---|\n155\t| E001 RHCL xKS support | E007, E010 | rewrite | If RHCL does not commit official xKS Helm charts: standalone Authorino + Limitador Helm deployment; contingency: direct Envoy ext_authz integration. Escalate to product leadership per strategy. |\n156\t| E002 OIDC feasibility on 3.4 codebase | E005 (rewrite), E006 (remove) | rewrite / remove | E006 fallback: if Tenant CRD is frozen, document the required OIDC group claim format and push IdP configuration burden to customers; record as a known limitation. |\n157\t| E003 CKS observability infrastructure | E009 | add_remediation | If CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, provision a dedicated Prometheus/Loki stack for the MaaS namespace before integration. |\n158\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dc1998c3ea04559","agent_id":"ad6f88bf8523a0ee8","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"c4c7fb53-3b65-4c69-a0d3-fee3a0f203a5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:48:03.814Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-95780dca8cfe7157","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
