{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af590350640260b8","type":"tool_result","content":"skills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:38:- **Triage inconsistency** (`triage: below-threshold` or `docs-only` with `epic_count > 1`): The decomposer applied multi-epic logic after triage should have stopped the flow. Merge all epics back into a single epic `{ID}-E001.md`. Combine the scope, acceptance criteria, and HLR traceability from all existing epics into the single file. Delete the extra epic files and their AI signal files. Update the decomposition summary to reflect `epic_count: 1` and `critical_path_length: 1`.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:80:python3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/{ID}-decomposition.md revised=true epic_count=<N> critical_path_length=<N>\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:55:- **2**: No circular dependencies. Every blocking edge is justified by the DAG construction rules. Critical path length is reasonable for strategy size. Epic frontmatter `dependencies` match the decomposition summary DAG.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:56:- **1**: Minor issues — an unjustified blocking edge that doesn't materially affect execution order, or critical path slightly longer than expected.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:59:Check: Trace the dependency graph. Verify each edge against the DAG construction rules (boundary rules 1-3, investigation edges 4-5, implementation type ordering 6-12, implementation edges 13-16, external dependency edges 17-19, generation rules 20-23, AC rules 24-26). Check that parallel-eligible work (different repos, no shared artifacts) is not unnecessarily serialized. **Implementation-type parallelism (rules 7, 8, 10) — enumerate and check every pair:** epics whose `implementation_type` pairing is mandated parallel must NOT have a blocking edge between them in either direction. Check **every** `konflux-onboarding` epic against **every** general-implementation epic (a decomposition often has more than one general epic — e.g. a component controller AND an OLM/CSV packaging epic; check all of them, not just the most obvious one). Specifically, a `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (rule 10), `license-validation → repo-onboarding` (rule 7), or `license-validation → konflux-onboarding` (rule 8) is a **critical** issue — score this criterion 0. The only permitted coupling between a `konflux-onboarding` epic and its component's general implementation is the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion, never a `dependencies`/DAG edge. Do not accept a rationale that the code \"needs the pipeline first.\" **In particular, an edge justified only by an image digest / SHA / `RELATED_IMAGE` / endpoint URL is a configuration reference (Rule 13), which must be an AC gate, not a `dependencies` edge — flag it as critical, do not accept it as an \"artifact dependency.\"** **Conditional branch files (`{ID}-BRANCH-<letter>-E*.md`), when present:** each MUST set both `branch` (the outcome letter, matching the filename) and `gated_by` (the main-plan Investigation `epic_id` that selects it) — a branch file missing either is a **critical** issue because the pipeline silently drops it. And no main-plan epic may list a branch epic in its `dependencies`: a main-plan epic depending on a `-BRANCH-` node (e.g. main-plan `E004` → `BRANCH-A-E003`) references an epic that is never created as an issue, a **critical** issue — work that hinges on the outcome must depend on the Investigation epic itself, and work that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch. Note: Rule 11 edges (all implementations → `docs-authoring`) are valid DAG edges but do not trigger priority inheritance — do not flag them as unjustified serialization or priority inheritance violations. Verify critical path length against strategy size heuristics (S: 1-2, M standard: 3-4, M with new component: 4-5, L: 5-7). **Cross-check consistency**: The DAG diagram convention is `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\"). Verify that every edge in the decomposition summary DAG diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. If arrows are drawn in the opposite direction (dependent → dependency), flag as a critical issue — the diagram is misleading even if frontmatter is correct. Any mismatch (edge in diagram but not in frontmatter, or frontmatter dependency referencing a nonexistent epic) is a critical issue — score 0. **Cross-check completeness**: also scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:28:    parent_strat=\"{ID}\" epic_count=1 critical_path_length=1 \\\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:256:    parent_strat=\"{ID}\" epic_count=<N> critical_path_length=<N>\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:373:Document branches in the decomposition summary. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path; `epic_count` may count either main-plan epics only or all epics including branches.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dc1998c3ea04559","agent_id":"ad6f88bf8523a0ee8","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"d9f91e2c-8d6e-4fb3-a33c-1ceafc47089d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:52:20.037Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-af590350640260b8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
