{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3c666e4e43dcb0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 15\n4\tcritical_path_length: 6\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8003 Decomposition — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: validate MaaS deployment on xKS (CKS priority, then AKS) as a Dev Preview, with external OIDC authentication replacing OpenShift identity infrastructure, Helm-based installation replacing OLM, and observability (metrics, logging, tracing) as a core value proposition.\n13\t\n14\t**Staff Engineer Input note:** The Staff Engineer Input section of the strategy contains only template placeholder text (no authored content), so no separate overrides were parsed. However, the strategy body explicitly embeds prior staff engineer guidance — CKS as priority platform, observability as core value proposition, and RHCL xKS support as a strategic dependency requiring Sprint 1 validation — and that guidance was treated as primary direction throughout.\n15\t\n16\t**Step 0 triage:** Not below-threshold (strategy is M-sized, spans multiple components and three teams, with multiple architectural sub-systems: controller code, API code, Helm packaging, observability, cross-team validation). Not docs-only (substantive code and configuration changes required). Full decomposition applies.\n17\t\n18\t## Component Mappings\n19\t\n20\t| Strategy component | Canonical component (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) | Notes |\n21\t|---|---|---|\n22\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing) | Model as a Service | Direct match; component exists in architecture context (`models-as-a-service.md`) |\n23\t| Kuadrant (Authorino, Limitador) | Inference Gateway | No canonical \"Kuadrant\" component; Kuadrant provides gateway-level authn/authz and rate limiting for the MaaS/AI gateway — closest parent-level component. Logged as non-obvious mapping |\n24\t| Observability (Prometheus, Perses, Loki) | Model and Agent Observability | MaaS-specific observability work; closest canonical match |\n25\t| RHAII installation tooling (xKS Platform) | AI Core Platform | RHAII xKS deployment tooling is not in the component list; closest parent-level platform component. Logged as non-obvious mapping |\n26\t\n27\t**Component existence (architecture context):** `models-as-a-service.md` exists (in platform). Kuadrant/RHCL has no architecture-context file of its own (referenced within `models-as-a-service.md`, `PLATFORM.md`, `ai-gateway-operator.md`) — it is an external RHCL/OpenShift-owned dependency, which is why the RHCL xKS support question is modeled as a blocking Investigation rather than an onboarding chain. Gateway API on CKS is confirmed available (Istio on CoreWeave). CKS Prometheus/Loki is assumed but unvalidated — modeled as a blocking Investigation.\n28\t\n29\t## Epic List\n30\t\n31\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n32\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 | RHCL official xKS support status and deployment mechanism validation | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 | External OIDC integration feasibility on the MaaS 3.4 codebase | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 | CKS observability infrastructure accessibility validation | Investigation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 | MaaS Helm chart packaging for xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 | maas-api external OIDC integration and OpenShift-assumption removal | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | maas-controller configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) deployment on CKS | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | maas-api observability instrumentation and Perses dashboards | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | CKS observability integration (Prometheus, Loki, Perses) | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | Structured logging for Authorino and Limitador | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P1 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | MaaS on CKS full-stack deployment validation | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E012 | MaaS on AKS deployment validation | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E013 | Distributed tracing through the MaaS request path | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E014 | MaaS Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS installation tooling | Implementation | xKS Platform | P1 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E015 | MaaS xKS documentation: known limitations and user identity flow | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n48\t\n49\tE015 priority note: `docs-authoring` priority exception applied — derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Blocker` (Blocker ≥ Critical → P0), not from its underlying P1/P2 HLRs, and it does not propagate upstream.\n50\t\n51\t## Dependency DAG\n52\t\n53\t```mermaid\n54\tgraph TD\n55\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 RHCL xKS support Investigation]\n56\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 OIDC feasibility Investigation]\n57\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 CKS observability Investigation]\n58\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 Helm chart packaging]\n59\t    E008[RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 maas-api observability]\n60\t    E001 --> E007\n61\t    E001 --> E010\n62\t    E002 --> E005\n63\t    E002 --> E006\n64\t    E003 --> E009\n65\t    E003 --> E013\n66\t    E004 --> E011\n67\t    E004 --> E014\n68\t    E005 --> E011\n69\t    E006 --> E011\n70\t    E007 --> E011\n71\t    E008 --> E009\n72\t    E009 --> E015\n73\t    E009 --> E010\n74\t    E010 --> E015\n75\t    E011 --> E009\n76\t    E011 --> E012\n77\t    E011 --> E013\n78\t    E012 --> E015\n79\t    E013 --> E015\n80\t    E014 --> E015\n81\t    E004 --> E015\n82\t    E005 --> E015\n83\t    E006 --> E015\n84\t    E007 --> E015\n85\t    E008 --> E015\n86\t    E011 --> E015\n87\t```\n88\t\n89\tRoots: E001, E002, E003, E004, E008 (all start immediately). Critical path: E001 → E007 → E011 → E009 → E010 → E015 (length 6; the E002 → E005/E006 → E011 → E009 → E010 → E015 chains are also length 6). Note: the E003 → E013 and E009 → E010 edges added in revision (see Revision Notes) lengthen the critical path from 5 to 6 — the strategy spans three teams and 15 epics (L-band, heuristic 5-7), and the alternative (leaving the E009/E010 log-pipeline interlock unordered) leaves acceptance criteria unmeetable, so the edge is the correct trade.\n90\t\n91\t## DAG Justification\n92\t\n93\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n94\t|---|---|---|\n95\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | RHCL support commitment determines the Kuadrant deployment mechanism (RHCL Helm charts vs standalone Authorino+Limitador vs direct Envoy ext_authz). True gate, action=rewrite. |\n96\t| E001 → E010 | Rule 3 | The selected deployment mechanism determines where structured logging for auth/rate-limit enforcement is configured. True gate, action=rewrite. |\n97\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | Whether the existing MaaS AuthPolicy can reuse external OIDC as-is or needs changes determines the maas-api change scope. True gate, action=rewrite. |\n98\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 | If the Tenant CRD is frozen in MaaS 3.4 GA, the groupClaimPath code change is dropped in favor of documenting the required claim format. True gate, action=remove. |\n99\t| E003 → E009 | Rule 3 / Rule 22 | If CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, the integration epic grows provisioning scope. True gate, action=add_remediation. |\n100\t| E003 → E013 | Rule 3 / Rule 22 | The CKS observability infrastructure validation determines whether an OTel collector is available on CKS for trace export; if not, the tracing epic grows collector-provisioning scope. True gate, action=add_remediation. |\n101\t| E004 → E011 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumer) | CKS validation deploys the Helm chart produced by the packaging epic — the chart is a build-against artifact. |\n102\t| E004 → E014 | Rule 12/13 | RHAII xKS installation tooling consumes the Helm chart artifact. |\n103\t| E005 → E011 | Rule 12 | CKS validation validates the OIDC-integrated maas-api build. |\n104\t| E006 → E011 | Rule 12 | CKS validation validates group-claim-driven tier mapping. |\n105\t| E007 → E011 | Rule 16 (external dependency gates Tier 2) | Full-stack validation requires working auth/rate-limiting enforcement on CKS. |\n106\t| E008 → E009 | Rule 12 | CKS integration scrapes the metrics that instrumentation emits; dashboards render those metric names. |\n107\t| E011 → E009 | Rule 12 | Observability integration ACs are measured against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS (\"Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with Prometheus integration...\"). |\n108\t| E009 → E010 | Rule 12 (artifact consumer) | The RHCL structured logging epic's ACs are measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki (per the strategy's logging AC), and its shipping configuration consumes the ingestion path and label scheme the CKS observability integration publishes — so it consumes that epic's aggregation surface. Ownership boundary: emission and shipping configuration for Authorino/Limitador logs live in E010; the aggregation surface, label scheme, and shipping for MaaS-owned component logs live in E009. The edge runs in this direction only: E009's own ACs cover the aggregation surface and maas-api logs (whose emission, E008, is already upstream), so the P1 logging epic does not block the P0 CKS integration epic — adding the reverse edge instead would both create a cycle with this data flow and make E010's strategy-mandated Loki-measured ACs unmeetable at its own completion. |\n109\t| E011 → E012 | Strategy sequencing | Strategy: validate on CKS first (priority platform), then AKS. AKS validation reuses the validated chart, OIDC configuration, and Kuadrant mechanism. |\n110\t| E011 → E013 | Rule 12 | Tracing requires the full request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) running on CKS. |\n111\t| E004…E014 → E015 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built and validated. Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n112\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ E005/E006 | Rules 14/15, 13 | Helm packaging and OIDC code changes are parallel work streams; image references are configuration references filled at deploy time (AC gates, not DAG edges). |\n113\t| (no edge) E008 ∥ E004/E005/E006 | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas (observability instrumentation vs packaging vs auth path) — coordination risk only, not dependency. |\n114\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002/E003/E004/E008 | Rule 17 / Tier 1 | Tier 1 independent work proceeds while the RHCL dependency is resolved. |\n115\t\n116\t**Conditional branches considered and rejected:** The RHCL deployment mechanism has three bounded outcomes (RHCL Helm charts / standalone Authorino+Limitador / direct Envoy ext_authz), but the strategy frames them as a priority-ordered fallback ladder whose deliverable — working Authorino + Limitador auth and rate limiting on CKS — is identical in every outcome; only the mechanism differs. Per Rule 21's fallback principle, the fallbacks are recorded as acceptance criteria and `gate_failure_impact` on E007 rather than as separate branch epics. This also avoids dead-locking the main-plan CKS validation epic (which needs the deployment under every outcome) against non-main-plan branch nodes.\n117\t\n118\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n119\t\n120\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n121\t|---|---|---|\n122\t| MaaS successfully deployed on CKS (priority platform) | P0 | E004, E011 (E014 supports) |\n123\t| MaaS successfully deployed on AKS (\"AKS AND CKS\") | P0 | E012 |\n124\t| Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | E007, E011 |\n125\t| External OIDC authentication validated for user identity | P0 | E002, E005, E011 |\n126\t| API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E005, E011 |\n127\t| Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E002, E006 |\n128\t| RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001, E007 |\n129\t| Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E003, E008, E009 |\n130\t| Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E006, E011 |\n131\t| Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E008 (maas-api emission), E010 (Authorino/Limitador emission and shipping), E009 (Loki aggregation surface, label scheme, and MaaS-component shipping) |\n132\t| Known limitations documented | P1 | E015 |\n133\t| User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E015 |\n134\t| Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E013 |\n135\t\n136\tAll 13 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n137\t\n138\t## Tiered Delivery\n139\t\n140\tThe RHCL official xKS support dependency has uncertain timing (external team commitment, Sprint 1 validation). Partial value is deliverable without it, so the strategy splits into:\n141\t\n142\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts immediately):** E002, E003, E004, E005, E006, E008, E014 — Helm packaging, OIDC integration code, observability instrumentation, and the investigations themselves. These deliver risk-reduction and the xKS-ready MaaS artifacts regardless of the RHCL outcome.\n143\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E007, E010, E011, E012, E013, E015 — Kuadrant deployment on CKS, structured logging for RHCL components, full-stack CKS/AKS validation, tracing, and final documentation. Gated (directly or transitively) on E001. E010 is additionally downstream of E009 (it ships into and is measured against the CKS Loki aggregation); E013 is additionally gated on E003 (OTel collector availability).\n144\t\n145\tThe MaaS 3.4 GA stable-build timing (internal dependency, \"critical blocker\" for Summit) does not split tiers: no work can be validated without a build, and the strategy's mitigations (RC build by April 27, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are timing tactics, not structural alternatives. Recorded as a health warning and an acceptance-criteria gate on the validation epics.\n146\t\n147\t## Health Warnings\n148\t\n149\t1. **Docs epic priority inversion (deliberate):** E015 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Blocker` → P0) even though its underlying HLRs are P1 (known limitations) and P2 (identity flow). Flagged for human review of whether a P0 docs epic is intended for a Dev Preview.\n150\t2. **MaaS 3.4 GA build availability:** code freeze passed late March 2026; stable-build delivery timing is unknown and is a Summit-timeline risk. Not structurally modelable — recorded as an AC gate (use RC build; RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) on E011/E012.\n151\t3. **Pending process gates:** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding (Helm chart build pipeline), License Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy prerequisites. Helm chart build pipeline onboarding is an AC on E004; license verification of pulled images (Authorino, Limitador, Perses) is an AC on E007/E009; the rest are program-level gates outside Dev Preview scope per the strategy.\n152\t4. **Observability instrumentation freeze risk:** if maas-api is frozen at 3.4 GA, metrics instrumentation may need a post-3.4 patch or deferral of logging to P1 (strategy assumption \"Needs validation\"). Recorded as an assumption/AC on E008 rather than an Investigation — the epic exists either way; only landing timing changes.\n153\t5. **AKS GatewayClass choice** (`envoy-gateway` vs `azure-application-gateway`) is an implementation detail deferred to E012 — not a genuine unknown (either works; the strategy lists both as validated options).\n154\t6. **Shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL** for MaaS on xKS is a configuration decision (xKS Platform owns); captured as a configurable Helm value and AC on E004, not an Investigation.\n155\t\n156\t## Investigation Gate Summary\n157\t\n158\t| Investigation | Gated epics | Gate action | Fallback approach |\n159\t|---|---|---|---|\n160\t| E001 RHCL xKS support | E007, E010 | rewrite | If RHCL does not commit official xKS Helm charts: standalone Authorino + Limitador Helm deployment; contingency: direct Envoy ext_authz integration. Escalate to product leadership per strategy. |\n161\t| E002 OIDC feasibility on 3.4 codebase | E005 (rewrite), E006 (remove) | rewrite / remove | E006 fallback: if Tenant CRD is frozen, document the required OIDC group claim format and push IdP configuration burden to customers; record as a known limitation. |\n162\t| E003 CKS observability infrastructure | E009, E013 | add_remediation | If CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, provision a dedicated Prometheus/Loki stack for the MaaS namespace before integration. If no OTel collector is available on CKS, the tracing epic provisions a dedicated collector endpoint for the MaaS namespace. |\n163\t\n164\t## Revision Notes\n165\t\n166\tApplied from the adversarial review (score 10/14, revise):\n167\t\n168\t1. **E009/E010 log-pipeline interlock (critical):** The review found E009's Loki AC required Authorino/Limitador structured logs that only E010 produces, with no DAG path between them, while E010's own ACs are measured by querying the Loki aggregation only E009 provides — a dependency running both ways. Resolved by fixing the ownership boundary (review issue on Epic Boundaries) rather than adding a cycle: E010 now explicitly owns emission *and* shipping configuration for Authorino/Limitador logs; E009 owns the aggregation surface, label scheme, and shipping for MaaS-owned component logs, and its logging AC was narrowed accordingly (maas-api logs, whose emission E008 already provides upstream). A single edge E009 → E010 captures the remaining data flow (shipping target and Loki-measured ACs). This also removes the P1-logging-ACs-inside-P0-epic tension: no P1 epic blocks a P0 epic's acceptance criteria, and no priority changes were needed.\n169\t2. **E003 → E013 (major):** E013's trace-export scope consumes the OTel-collector availability determination from the E003 investigation. E003 added to E013's dependencies with `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` (action=add_remediation: provision a dedicated collector if none is available on CKS).\n170\t3. **E014 fallback ladder (minor):** E014's scope and assumptions now state explicitly that the Kuadrant prerequisite is encoded as a configurable prerequisite covering the investigation's bounded fallback ladder, so no edge to E001/E007 is required.\n171\t4. **E013 AI signals (minor):** `open_questions` and `external_dependency` corrected to -1 (gated on an open investigation; CKS-hosted collector infrastructure), with matching rationale in the ai-signals file. `ai_implementability` left for the pipeline to recompute.\n172\t5. **PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation (minor):** The strategy's in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5 min) for validated API key hashes is now implemented by E005 (scope bullet plus a cache-hit/cache-miss AC derived from the strategy's failure-mode criterion); E011's PostgreSQL-unavailability AC exercises it end-to-end and its risk entry no longer says \"if implemented\".\n173\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6290c29df86f11f","agent_id":"a91fef4195d5ac548","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"107fd454-7d1a-4e14-8bd5-a61a432ee767","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:56:03.003Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e3c666e4e43dcb0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
