{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-be99724591cd3cb5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E005\n3\ttitle: maas-api external OIDC integration and OpenShift-assumption removal\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the existing MaaS AuthPolicy cannot reuse external OIDC as-is,\n15\t    expand scope to the enumerated code changes in MaaSAuthPolicy generation for Authorino's\n16\t    oidc identity source; if reuse is configuration-only, scope shrinks to configuration\n17\t    and internal middleware adaptation.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 5\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# maas-api external OIDC integration and OpenShift-assumption removal\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tAdapt the maas-api component for external OIDC authentication on xKS: configure the authentication path to use a customer-provided OIDC identity provider (via Authorino's `oidc` identity source) instead of OpenShift's Kubernetes TokenReview identity mapping, and remove OpenShift-specific assumptions from the API plane. The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*` via the maas-api callback validation endpoint) is OIDC-agnostic and must continue to work unchanged.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Configure the MaaS authentication path to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, driven by the Tenant CRD's existing external OIDC configuration fields (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`) — per the strategy, basic OIDC integration is expected to be a configuration change rather than a code change, to be confirmed by the OIDC feasibility investigation.\n44\t- Adapt or gate the maas-api internal authentication middleware (TenantAuthMiddleware, which validates bearer tokens via Kubernetes TokenReview and SubjectAccessReview) for environments without OpenShift identity mapping, on the internal API paths not covered by the gateway AuthPolicy.\n45\t- Remove OpenShift-specific assumptions from maas-api configuration (identity infrastructure, certificate conventions).\n46\t- Preserve the API key validation callback path (`/internal/v1/api-keys/validate`) unchanged — it is OIDC-agnostic by design.\n47\t- Implement the strategy's PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation in the API key validation path: an in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 minutes) for validated API key hashes, so validation of recently used API keys survives a PostgreSQL outage within the TTL window. Additive and configuration-driven; no behavioral change when PostgreSQL is available.\n48\t- Ensure all changes are additive or configuration-driven: RHOAI MaaS GA behavior with OpenShift identity must be unchanged (strategy NFR: backwards compatibility).\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n53\t- Given the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed (API key validation is OIDC-independent), while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n54\t- Given a MaaS deployment on xKS, API key authentication (the `sk-oai-*` flow) functions without any OpenShift identity infrastructure, measured by successful authenticated requests.\n55\t- External OIDC authentication is validated with at least one standards-compliant OIDC provider (e.g. Keycloak).\n56\t- Given the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable (network partition or pod restart), inference requests using API keys validated within the last 5 minutes continue to succeed (in-memory LRU cache hit), while validation of API keys not in cache fails (cache miss scenario), and API key creation fails with HTTP 503, measured by response status and error logs during a controlled PostgreSQL outage.\n57\t- No behavioral change to RHOAI MaaS GA when OpenShift identity is present (additive/config-driven only).\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n62\t- [P0] API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS.\n63\t\n64\t## Dependencies and Gating\n65\t\n66\t- Gated by the OIDC integration feasibility investigation on the MaaS 3.4 codebase: if the existing AuthPolicy cannot reuse external OIDC as-is, this epic's scope expands to the enumerated code changes; if reuse is configuration-only, scope shrinks accordingly.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6290c29df86f11f","agent_id":"a91fef4195d5ac548","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"3e35bd61-65d5-42f6-8990-3cd5531c06d1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:56:12.769Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be99724591cd3cb5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
