th exceeded quota identifiers (user, model, subscription as applicable).\n45\t- Own the log shipping configuration for Authorino and Limitador logs into CKS Grafana Loki: configure log output and shipping for these RHCL-owned components using the ingestion path and label scheme published by the CKS observability integration work (which provides the Loki aggregation surface for the MaaS namespace). The label scheme is agreed with that work; shipping for MaaS-owned component logs stays with the CKS observability integration.\n46\t- Apply the logging configuration under the Kuadrant deployment mechanism selected by the RHCL xKS support investigation (official RHCL charts, standalone Helm, or direct Envoy ext_authz contingency — the last re-scopes this work to the corresponding auth components).\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with structured logging enabled, when an authentication event occurs in Authorino (success or failure), then the log entry includes timestamp, user identity, authentication method, decision, and reason, measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki with label filters.\n51\t- Given a user exceeds a configured rate limit, the Limitador log entry records the enforcement event with the exceeded quota identifiers, measured by Loki queries.\n52\t- Log volume and formatting do not impair the auth/rate-limiting data path (no synchronous blocking on log emission).\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- [P1] Observability logging functional: authentication events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection (per Staff Engineer Input guidance embedded in the strategy) — Authorino/Limitador portion.\n57\t\n58\t## Dependencies and Gating\n59\t\n60\t- Gated by the RHCL xKS support investigation: the selected deployment mechanism determines where and how structured logging is configured for the auth and rate-limiting components; under the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency this epic is rewritten for the corresponding components.\n61\t- Depends on the CKS observability integration work: its acceptance criteria are measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki, and its shipping configuration consumes the ingestion path and label scheme that work publishes. The dependency runs in this direction only — the CKS observability integration's own acceptance criteria cover the aggregation surface and MaaS-owned component logs, not this epic's output, so this P1 epic does not block the P0 CKS integration.\n62\t\n63\t## Risks\n64\t\n65\t- Changes to RHCL-owned components require coordination with the OpenShift RHCL team; if official xKS support is not committed, changes may need to go upstream or be configuration-only.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6290c29df86f11f","agent_id":"a91fef4195d5ac548","session_id":"b158df32-2239-4f0e-abf0-6598f1f60a7a","uuid":"3bb8f969-984b-4396-833b-bb2e9a844a96","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:56:21.390Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f3990212ee29be9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
