{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b291163ff925d7b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E013\n3\ttitle: Distributed tracing through the MaaS request path\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P2\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E011\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n12\tai_signals:\n13\t  change_specificity: 1\n14\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n15\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n16\t  existing_foundation: 1\n17\t  open_questions: -1\n18\t  external_dependency: -1\n19\t  human_process_gates: 0\n20\t  repo_access: 1\n21\t  architecture_claims: 1\n22\timplementation_type: null\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 4\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n29\tgate_failure_impact:\n30\t  action: add_remediation\n31\t  fallback_approach: If the CKS observability infrastructure validation finds no OpenTelemetry\n32\t    collector available on CKS, add provisioning of a dedicated OTel collector endpoint\n33\t    for the MaaS namespace (or route trace export through the provisioned dedicated\n34\t    observability stack) before configuring trace export.\n35\t---\n36\t# Distributed tracing through the MaaS request path\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tEnable distributed tracing through the full MaaS request path on xKS — gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend — using OpenTelemetry, per the Staff Engineer Input guidance embedded in the strategy that observability is a core value proposition. P2 priority: deferrable beyond the Dev Preview core. maas-api already exports traces to an OpenTelemetry Collector via OTLP/gRPC (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md), so this extends existing foundations across the remaining hops.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Ensure W3C trace-context propagation across the request path: the gateway (Istio/Envoy on CKS), Authorino (ext_authz), Limitador (rate limiting), the payload-processing BBR ext_proc server, and the model backend.\n45\t- Configure trace export to an OpenTelemetry collector endpoint on CKS, whose availability is determined by the CKS observability infrastructure validation (this epic is gated on that investigation).\n46\t- Validate end-to-end traces for representative inference requests, including authenticated and rate-limited paths.\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with tracing enabled, when an inference request traverses gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend, then a single correlated trace spans all hops, measured by querying the trace backend for a representative request.\n51\t- Trace propagation does not break authentication or rate limiting enforcement.\n52\t- Tracing overhead does not materially degrade request latency on the Dev Preview workload.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- [P2] Distributed tracing functional: gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend (per Staff Engineer Input guidance embedded in the strategy).\n57\t\n58\t## Dependencies and Gating\n59\t\n60\t- Gated by the CKS observability infrastructure validation: it determines whether an OpenTelemetry collector is available on CKS for MaaS trace export. If none is available, this epic's scope grows to provisioning a dedicated OTel collector endpoint for the MaaS namespace (or routing trace export through the provisioned dedicated observability stack) before configuring export.\n61\t- Depends on the full request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) running on CKS, validated by the full-stack deployment validation work.\n62\t\n63\t## Assumptions\n64\t\n65\t- Tracing configuration for Authorino/Limitador depends on the Kuadrant deployment mechanism selected by the RHCL xKS support investigation.\n66\t"}]},"parent_to