E010's \"do not impair the data path\" are qualitative where the sibling observability epic (E008) sets a concrete bound (p99 ≤ 5ms) — tracing/logging deserve similar measurable thresholds.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: both platform validations (CKS priority, AKS), OIDC authentication and group-claim mapping, API key flow, subscription/tier management, rate limiting, all three observability tiers (P0 metrics, P1 logging, P2 tracing — each with its own epic or documented ownership portion), known-limitations and identity-flow documentation, all four failure-mode criteria (OIDC outage, Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability including the LRU-cache mitigation implemented in E005 and exercised end-to-end in E011/E012), the excluded items (operator installation, Dashboard UI) respected and documented as exclusions in E015, and the RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo carried as an AC gate on E011. Strategy-level context is carried forward: the MaaS 3.4 build-timing risk, RHCL escalation path, observability freeze risk, and Summit timeline all appear in the relevant epics or health warnings; the seven open questions are each resolved by an investigation, an AC, or a documented health warning (the OIDC-caching open question is settled by the strategy's own failure-mode criterion — OIDC requests fail 503 during IdP outage while API keys continue). A docs-authoring epic exists despite Documentation Support being only \"Pending human review\", which satisfies Rule 11 conservatively. Cross-epic consistency holds: every upstream multi-item scope (three MaaS components in E004, four infrastructure elements in E003, two platforms in the validation chain) is accounted for downstream.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72490,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6290c29df86f11f","agent_id":"a91fef4195d5ac548","session_id":"